    <div class="hbadge rv in">Journal · Nature</div>
    <h1 class="rv in" style="font-size:clamp(2.2rem,5vw,4rem);max-width:20ch">Are There Flying Fish in Madeira?</h1>
    <div class="artmeta rv in d1"><span>2 December 2026</span><span>5 min read</span><span>Funchal · Madeira</span></div>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="pad">
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="article rv">
      <p class="lede">Yes. Flying fish live in the open Atlantic around Madeira, and whale watching crews record them often enough to list them among the animals they see most regularly. From a moving boat they appear as small silver shapes bursting out of the water and gliding low over the waves for tens of metres.</p>

      <h2>What kind of flying fish live around Madeira?</h2><p>Mostly four-winged flying fish of the genus <em>Cheilopogon</em>. Local whale watching guides say most of the ones they see are hard to name to species from a boat, but the Atlantic flying fish, <em>Cheilopogon melanurus</em>, is the one they mention most. Other species turn up too: a margined flying fish was photographed off Calheta in December 2021.</p><p>There is a nice local link in the name. The genus <em>Cheilopogon</em> was described in 1841 by the naturalist Richard Thomas Lowe, in a paper on the fishes of Madeira. So the scientific name of the flying fish you might see off Cabo Girão was coined from fish studied on this island.</p><p>Flying fish are a family of their own, the Exocoetidae, with roughly 40 to 50 species worldwide in warm and temperate seas. The Atlantic flying fish is usually about 15 cm long and reaches about 30 cm. "Four-winged" means that both pairs of fins, the long ones behind the head and the smaller pair further back, are enlarged into gliding surfaces.</p><h2>Do they really fly?</h2><p>No, they glide. A flying fish never flaps its fins. It swims hard towards the surface with its fins folded tight against its body, breaks out, spreads them, and sails a little above the water. When it starts to drop, it can dip its tail back into the sea and beat it fast, like an outboard motor, to gain speed and launch a second glide without going back under.</p><p>The numbers are impressive for an animal the size of a sardine:</p><ul><li>A typical glide covers about 30 to 50 metres.</li><li>The strongest fliers can cover around 180 metres in a single glide.</li><li>Linked glides, with tail kicks in between, have been timed at up to 43 seconds and about 400 metres.</li></ul><p>The point of all this is escape. Under the water they are chased by tuna, swordfish and dolphins. In the air they get a few seconds of safety, which is often enough.</p><h2>When can you see flying fish in Madeira?</h2><p>Any time of year, as far as anyone has recorded. There is no official monthly count for Madeira, but boat logs from local whale watching trips mention flying fish in February, April, June and September, and the Calheta photograph was taken in December. So it is not a summer-only sighting.</p><p>What matters more than the month is the sea state. On a calm, sunny day with a gentle swell, a silver fish skimming the surface is easy to pick out. On a choppy day with whitecaps everywhere, you can sail past dozens and never notice them.</p><h2>What do they look like from a moving boat?</h2><p>Like a skimming stone that forgot to sink. Usually you see a flick of silver to the side of the bow, then a small shape with its fins spread like a dragonfly, gliding low and dead straight across the waves. It may tilt, touch the water, kick and keep going. Then it drops back in with a tiny splash and is gone. Sometimes several launch at once and scatter in different directions.</p><p>The boat itself is often what sets them off. The noise and the bow wave of a vessel moving at speed are exactly the kind of threat they jump away from, so the fast stretches of a trip are often the best time to see them.</p><p>Seabirds know this trick too. Cory's shearwaters, the large brown and white seabirds that glide low over Madeira's waters, join dolphins in hunting flying fish. When dolphins push a school to the surface and the birds wait above, the fish are caught between the two. If you see shearwaters circling and diving low in one spot, it is worth watching that patch of water.</p><h2>How do you spot one on a boat trip?</h2><p>A few simple habits raise your chances:</p><ol><li>Sit or stand where you can see forward and to the side, and keep your eyes 20 to 50 metres ahead of the bow, not straight down.</li><li>Wear sunglasses, ideally polarised. They cut the glare on the water and make silver shapes stand out.</li><li>Watch hardest during the faster parts of the trip, when the boat startles fish ahead of it.</li><li>Look where seabirds are gathering low over the water.</li><li>Do not try to photograph the first one. Watch it, learn the rhythm, and have the camera ready in burst mode for the next.</li></ol><h2>Can you see flying fish on a Chifbay trip?</h2><p>You might. Nobody can promise a wild animal, and Chifbay does not. Flying fish live in open water, and the south coast route from Funchal to Cabo Girão and beyond runs over deep water close to shore, so they can appear anywhere along it. On the <a href="/hidden-coves-half-day">Day Trip</a>, the fast run back to Funchal at the end is a good moment to watch ahead of the bow. On the <a href="/sunset-cruise">Sunset Trip</a>, your best chance is on the way out, while there is still enough light to follow a small silver shape over the water.</p><p>Be clear about what you are after. If you want the best chance of dolphins, whales and the animals that hunt flying fish, a dedicated whale watching trip, which goes offshore to search for them, is the better choice. A private coastal trip shows you the cliffs, the swims and whatever the sea offers on the way. For the bigger picture, read our guides to <a href="/posts/dolphins-and-whales-in-madeira-guide">dolphins and whales in Madeira</a> and to the <a href="/posts/top-10-marine-animals-madeira">marine animals you can see in Madeira's waters</a>.</p><h2>Do flying fish ever land on boats?</h2><p>Yes, now and then. At night they are drawn to boat lights and can jump straight onto a low deck. In daylight it is rarer, but a fish that misjudges its glide can end up on board. If it happens, wet your hands and put it back in the sea quickly. Atlantic flying fish are listed as Least Concern, and they are not a commercial catch in Europe, so the one on your deck is simply having a bad day.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>What is a flying fish called in Portuguese?</h3><p>Peixe-voador, which means flying fish.</p><h3>How far can a flying fish glide?</h3><p>Usually 30 to 50 metres. The strongest can glide about 180 metres, and linked glides have reached around 400 metres.</p><h3>Are flying fish dangerous?</h3><p>No. They are small, harmless fish that eat plankton and tiny fish. The only risk is one landing on a low deck at night.</p><h3>Are flying fish a sign that dolphins are near?</h3><p>Not always, but dolphins and seabirds do hunt them, so a lot of flying fish and shearwaters in one area is worth watching.</p>

      <blockquote>Keep your eyes fifty metres ahead of the bow on the run home, and the sea sometimes throws a small silver dart.</blockquote>
    </article>
    <p class="pref-src" data-pref-source><span>Enjoying our Madeira guides?</span> <a href="https://www.google.com/preferences/source?q=chifbay.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="#4285F4" d="M23.5 12.3c0-.8-.1-1.6-.2-2.3H12v4.5h6.5a5.6 5.6 0 0 1-2.4 3.6v3h3.9c2.2-2.1 3.5-5.1 3.5-8.8z"/><path fill="#34A853" d="M12 24c3.2 0 6-1.1 7.9-2.9l-3.9-3c-1.1.7-2.4 1.2-4 1.2-3.1 0-5.7-2.1-6.6-4.9h-4v3.1A12 12 0 0 0 12 24z"/><path fill="#FBBC05" d="M5.4 14.4a7.2 7.2 0 0 1 0-4.7V6.6h-4a12 12 0 0 0 0 10.8l4-3z"/><path fill="#EA4335" d="M12 4.8c1.7 0 3.3.6 4.6 1.8l3.4-3.4A12 12 0 0 0 1.4 6.6l4 3.1C6.3 6.9 8.9 4.8 12 4.8z"/></svg>Add Chifbay as a preferred source on Google</a></p>

